\fs22 \cf0 \'93These are the five output classes that the current model identifies a comment as. If the statement is an unclear comment, it\'92s given a score of 0. If it\'92s a short question or some technical clarification, it gives a score of 1. And so on\'85 If a comment demonstrates an elaboration in solution or deep analysis of the problem, we\'92ll score that as a 4. We\'92ll be using this same metric to analyze Dr. Thad\'92s piazza data. Now I\'92ll elaborate on the research goals for Cognitive Science Modeling.
\fs24 \
\'a0\

\fs22 Our overall approach for this modeling would be to predict cognitive presence in Piazza comments from Dr. Thad\'92s course. Last semester, the team was able to achieve a score of 80% on the training set with the BERT model, but there were some misclassifications in the test set and needed to consider a different metric for the model. For this semester, we\'92ll be working with a different dataset, but with the same model. We would focus more on cleaner extraction of comments from the dataset, the results of running the model on this data and integration of the results with the Web-Development team. Once this exploration is complete, and if time permits, we will search for ways to improve the accuracy of our model. Now I\'92ll talk about the Sentiment Analysis for the actual posts.
\fs24 \
\'a0\

\fs22 For the first half of the semester, we would be focusing on sentiment analysis of the Piazza comments for professors to understand their class requirements. Last semester, the team conducted the analysis using a pre-existing VADER model in the NLTK package in Python, but due to the limited availability of the data this could not be integrated with the other team. For this semester, our goal for the first half of the semester is to search for a better model, if any, to perform this analysis on data from Dr. Thad\'92s course. Then results will be provided to the web-dev team.\'94
